# Fluorine-induced surface modification to obtain stable and low energy loss zinc oxide/perovskite interface for photovoltaic application

Vignesh Murugadoss1 $\cdot$ Dae Yun Kang1 $\cdot$ Won Jun Lee1 $\cdot$ Il Gyu Jang1 $\cdot$ Tae Geun Kim1
Abstract
Despite its superior optoelectronic properties, the use of zinc oxide $(Z\mathrm{nO})$ as an electron transport layer (ETL) is limited compared to that of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ due to its chemical instability with perovskite. Although several approaches have been presented to alleviate this instability, the use of all-metal halides has not been reported. In this study, we develop stable $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}/$ perovskite interfaces through a single-step post-annealing surface modification. The $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ surface is modified using ammonium fluoride $\mathrm{(NH}_{4}\mathrm{F)}$ solutions with different concentrations, thereby optimizing the ETL/perovskite interface to simultaneously reduce the loss and increase the stability while maintaining the photoconversion efficiency. Furthermore, the influence of the fluorine concentration on the physical, chemical, optical, and electrical properties of $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ thin films is investigated. The photovoltaic performance of the planar perovskite solar cell fabricated using the surface-modified $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ , as the ETL is nearly three times higher than that of the cell obtained using the control. The proposed technique can serve as a facile and holistic approach to enhance the chemical stability and device performance in photovoltaic and optoelectronic applications, irrespective of the ETL and perovskite materials.
Keywords  Surface modification $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{\varepsilon}$ Fluorination $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{\varepsilon}$ Stable interface $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{\varepsilon}$ Perovskite $\cdot$ Solar cell
## 1  Introduction
Organic-inorganic metal halide perovskites have emerged as promising materials for next-generation electronic and photonic devices because of their desirable properties such as a tunable bandgap, high absorption coefficient, long carrier diffusion length, defect tolerance, and low binding energy [1–5]. Notably, the power-conversion efficiency (PCE) of perovskite solar cells with an n-type metal oxide layer has been increased to over $25.2\%$ in a short span of two decades [6, 7]. Among various architectures, planar n-i-p perovskite solar cells have attracted considerable attention as they can be simply and inexpensively processed to enable large-scale production [8, 9]. However, the degradation of perovskite at the electron transport layer (ETL)/perovskite interface leads to inefficient charge transfer at the interfaces and a large energy loss [10–13]. Therefore, it is of significance to understand the degradation mechanism at the charge transport layer/perovskite interface. Although the ETL properties of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{{}}Z\mathrm{{nO}}$ have been noted to be superior than those of other metal oxides $(Z\mathsf{n}_{2}\mathsf{S n O}_{4}$ , $\mathbf{Nb}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{5}$ , ${\bf W O}_{3}$ , etc.), the perovskite instability and non-radiative recombination at the interfaces of such oxides remain to be addressed [14–20]. Compared to polycrystalline $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ , $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ exhibits higher optical transparency in the visible to IR region, wider tenability in electronic properties, higher mobility due to a lower effective electron mass, and shallower conduction band edge that may align more closely to metal halide perovskites [21–30]. In addition, $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ can be crystallized at low temperatures. However, the use of $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ in perovskite solar cells has been limited due to stability issues [31–33]. In this context, it is necessary to investigate the degradation mechanism at $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}\it{I}}}$ perovskite interfaces and properly modify the interface to reduce the associated energy loss and enhance the stability.
Several degradation mechanisms have been reported to explain the $Z\mathrm{nO}$ -perovskite interface instability. Cheng et al. [34] noted that the chemisorbed hydroxide groups on the $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanoparticles induce the decomposition of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ and demonstrated that the perovskite stability can be increased by reducing the surface density of the hydroxide groups via high-temperature annealing. However, high-temperature annealing leads to a deterioration in the $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ properties. Schutt et  al. [35] experimentally demonstrated that the degradation of perovskite at the $\mathrm{ZnO}/$ $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ interface can be attributed to the deprotonation of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ by $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ and replaced $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ with $\mathrm{FA_{0.83}C S_{0.17}P b(I_{0.83}B r_{0.17})_{3}}$ to suppress the deprotonation of the cation. Replacing $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ with formamidinium $\mathrm{[CH(NH}_{2})_{2}]$ reduces the acidity of halide perovskites, thereby preventing the deprotonation of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ . However, the reliable manufacturing and/or processing of 3D perovskites with mixed cations or halide ions is challenging [34, 36, 37]. Jung et al. [38] reported that the replacement of hydroxyls on the surface of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ by fluorine can help reduce the energy loss in $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -based perovskite solar cells. Zhang et al. [39] passivated the surface of mesoporous $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ via chlorination to suppress charge recombination and facilitate charge transport at the perovskite $/Z\mathrm{nO}$ interface Furthermore, Wolf et al. [40] reported that the Lewis acidity of the $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ surface can be altered through fluorine treatment, with the modification effect depending on the fluorine concentration. The results from the abovementioned studies indicate that a decrease in the density of hydroxide groups on the $\mathrm{{}}Z\mathrm{{nO}}$ surface and the modification of the surface Lewis acidity of $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ can alleviate the instability at the ZnO/ $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ interface and energy loss. Recently, Zhang et al. [41] demonstrated that passivation by the Lewis base molecule, 2-mercaptopyridine (2-MP), can increase the stability of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ . Hence, it can be considered that the fluorination of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ surfaces can increase the efficiency and stability of perovskite solar cells. However, none of the existing studies has investigated the influence of surface fluorination on the charge transport properties and Lewis acidity of the $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ electron transport layer. Notably, the fluorine concentration on the $\mathrm{{}}Z\mathrm{{nO}}$ surface can probably significantly influence the charge transport properties of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ thin films and thereby affect the photovoltaic performance. However, this aspect has not been sufficiently investigated in the earlier reports. Considering these aspects, we report on the optimized surface fluorination of $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ thin films to enhance the $\mathrm{ZnO/CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ interface stability and performance of n-i-p perovskite solar cell.
In particular, $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ thin films are treated with ammonium fluoride $\mathrm{(NH}_{4}\mathrm{F)}$ to remove the hydroxyl groups and defects to increase the stability at the $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}\it{I}}}$ perovskite interface. Fluorine is expected to more effective than other halogen anions because of its ionic radius: The ionic radius of fluorine (1.33 Å) is nearly identical to that of oxygen $(1.40\mathring\mathrm{A})$ , which can help prevent the occurrence of lattice distortions in $\mathrm{{}}Z\mathrm{{nO}}$ . The experimental results highlight that surface fluorination reduces the Lewis basic nature of $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ thin films, thereby inhibiting the degradation of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ via deprotonation. Furthermore, the concentration of $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ is varied to tune the work function for efficient charge transport. The proposed approach to optimize the surface properties of ETLs enables a low-temperature processing and allows the use of metal halide perovskites.
## 2  Experimental details
## 2.1  Preparation of solutions for perovskite solar cells
$\mathbf{A}0.5\mathbf{M}Z\mathbf{n}0$ precursor solution was prepared by dissolving zinc acetate dihydrate $[\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO})_{2}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}]$ in 2-methoxyethanol. As a stabilizing agent, an equimolar ethanolamine solution was added, and the solution was heated at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ [42]. The precursor solution was stirred until a clear solution was obtained and filtered before use. The $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ solution was prepared by dissolving $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ in deionized water. A spiro-OMeTAD solution was prepared, as reported in an existing study [38]. Specifically, $1.1\mathrm{mL}$ of spiro-OMeTAD solution in chlorobenzene $(90.9\mathrm{mg/}$ mL) was prepared with the addition of $23\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of Li-TFSI solution in acetonitrile $(590\mathrm{mg/ml})$ ), $39\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of tBP, and $10\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of FK209 Co (II) TFSI salt solution in acetonitrile $(376\mathrm{mg/ml})$ as dopants.
## 2.2  Preparation of Ref. ZnO and $N H_{4}F$ ‑treated ZnO thin films
The glass substrates were cleaned sequentially in deionized water, acetone, and isopropyl alcohol via ultrasonication and treated with oxygen-plasma before the thin film deposition. The filtered $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ precursor solution was deposited on the glass substrates at $3000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $30\mathrm{~s~}$ and annealed at $180^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30\mathrm{min}$ to obtain the Ref. $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ thin films. The Ref. $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ thin films were directly treated with $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ by spin coating the $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ solution on a $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ substrate at $5000\mathrm{rpm}$ for $20~\mathrm{s}$ . Subsequently, the substrate was annealed at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30~\mathrm{min}$ . The Ref. $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ thin films were directly treated with the $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ solution. Different fluoride concentrations $(0.02\mathrm{~M},0.04\mathrm{~M},0.06\mathrm{~M}.$ $0.08{\bf M}$ , and $0.10\mathbf{M}$ in deionized water) were employed to vary the hydroxyl group concentrations on the surface of the $Z\mathrm{nO}$ thin films. The obtained thin films are labeled as $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ $(0.02{\bf M})$ , ZnO $(0.04~\mathrm{M})$ , ZnO (0.06 M), $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ (0.08 M), and ZnO (0.10 M). The Ref. $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ thin film $\mathbf{\zeta}^{\mathrm{ZnO)}}$ was used as the reference sample.
## 2.3  Characterization of Ref. ZnO and $N H_{4}F.$ ‑treated ZnO thin films
The phase analysis for $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ thin films was conducted using an X-ray diffractometer (Rigaku, SmartLab) with a $\mathrm{CuK}\upalpha$ radiation source having a scan rate of $0.02^{\circ}$ per second.
FTIR spectra were recorded in the wavenumber range of $2000{-}400\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (Thermo Fisher Scientific NICOLET iS10). The transmittance spectra of $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ thin films were recorded using an ultraviolet-visible (UV-Vis) spectrophotometer (lambda-35, Perkin Elmer). The surface morphology and surface roughness of the $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ thin films were observed using an atomic force microscope (Park system, XE-series) operated in the tapping mode. The chemical states of the $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ thin films were investigated through $X\mathrm{\Omega}$ -ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) (ULVAC-PHI, X-TOOL). The work function of the $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ thin films was obtained via ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (Theta probe base system, Thermo Fisher Scientific). The ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) source comes from a helium discharge lamp $(\mathrm{h}\nu=21.2\mathrm{eV})$ ). The electrical properties (I–V characteristics) were determined using a semiconductor parameter analyzer (Keithley 4200).
## 2.4  Fabrication of perovskite solar cells
Patterned ITO substrates were precleaned and treated with oxygen-plasma before the thin film deposition. The $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ precursor solution was deposited on the $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ layer at $4000\mathrm{rpm}$ for $30\mathrm{{s}}$ . During the spin coating process, anhydrous chlorobenzene was dropped after $10\mathrm{~s~}$ . Subsequently, the perovskite was annealed at $90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ . Next, the spiro-OMeTAD solution was deposited onto the perovskite surface at $4000\mathrm{rpm}$ . Finally, the silver electrode was deposited via thermal evaporation. The photovoltaic performance of the fabricated perovskite solar cells under illumination was evaluated using a Keithley 2401 digital source meter controlled by a commercial software (K730, McScience, Suwon, Republic of Korea). A light source integrated with a Xe lamp with an illumination power of $100\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ $\mathrm{\bf\langleAM}1.5\mathrm{\bfG}$ ) was used as the solar simulator (McScience, Suwon, Republic of Korea) and calibrated using a Si reference cell. The solar cell measurements were performed under an ambient atmosphere $(\mathrm{RH}30\mathrm{-}45\%)$ at room temperature. Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) and Tafel polarization measurements were performed under dark conditions. The EIS plots were recorded in the frequency range of $100\mathrm{kHz}$ to $1\mathrm{mHz}$ with an AC amplitude of $10\mathrm{mV}.$ . The Tafel plots were recorded in the voltage $-0.5$ V and $0.5\mathrm{V}$ at the scan rate of $25\mathrm{mVs^{-1}}$ .
## 3  Results and discussion
## 3.1  Influence of fluorine modification on structural and optical properties
The X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns for Ref. $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ and $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ -treated $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ thin films are shown in Fig. 1a. The diffraction peak corresponding to the (0 0 2) plane is predominant at a $2\theta$ of approximately $34^{\circ}$ for all the $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ thin films. Other weak peaks corresponding to the (1 1 0) and (1 1 2) planes can be observed at ${\sim}57.4^{\circ}$ and $67.3^{\circ}$ , respectively [43, 44]. The XRD patterns are well indexed with the hexagonal (wurtzite) crystal structure (ICDD PDF Card No. 01-074- 9940). The intensity of the diffraction peak corresponding to the (0 0 2) plane decreases for $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ $\left(0.08{\bf M}\right)$ and $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ (0.10 M) (Fig. 1b–d). This phenomenon indicates the suppression of $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ crystallization at higher concentrations of $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ . No secondary peaks can be observed, which demonstrates that the treatment with $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ does not lead to the formation of fluorine-related compounds. Furthermore, no significant shift occurs in the $\boldsymbol{\mathcal{Z}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{nO}}$ peaks because the ionic radius of fluorine $(r_{F^{-}}=1.33A)$ is similar to that oxygen $(r_{O^{-}}=1.40A)$ , which helps avoid any lattice distortion.
The influence of $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ treatment on the $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ thin film surface was qualitatively investigated through FTIR spectroscopy. The FTIR spectra of the untreated $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ thin film and $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ -treated $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ thin films are shown in Fig. 2a. An additional sharp peak can be observed at approximately $1384~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ , which can attribute to the $_\mathrm{H-O-H}$ bending vibration associated with a small amount of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in the $Z\mathrm{nO}$ nanocrystals. Furthermore, the peak assigned to $-\mathrm{OH}$ on the surface of the treated $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ thin film $(3314.91~\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ ) is shifted to a lower wavenumber $(3384.48~\mathrm{cm^{-1}},$ ) after the $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ treatment. In addition, the relative intensity of the band assigned to the $-\mathrm{OH}$ group on the surface decreases. These results indicate that the –OH bond is replaced by fluorine, which increases the Lewis acidity [40]. This increase in the Lewis acid nature of the $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ surface may suppress the deprotonation of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ , thereby enhancing the stability of the $\mathrm{ZnO}/$ perovskite interface [35]. Figure 2b shows the transmittance spectra of $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ thin films deposited on the glass substrate. The average transmittance of the $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ thin films before and after $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ treatment is $85\%$ in the visible region (from 400 to $700\mathrm{nm}$ ). No significant deviation occurs in the transmittance of the $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ thin films after the $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ treatment. This finding indicates that the $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ treatment does not affect the optical properties of $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ thin films [45].
## 3.2  Influence of fluorine modification on surface morphology
The surface morphologies of the $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ thin film and $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}.$ -treated $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ thin films, determined using a non-contact mode atomic force microscope, are shown in Fig. 3. The $\mathrm{{}}Z\mathrm{{nO}}$ and $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}.$ -treated $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ thin films are smooth and exhibit complete surface coverage. The root mean square values for $\mathrm{{}}Z\mathrm{{nO}}$ (0.02 M), $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ ${\bf\nabla}(0.04{\bf M})$ , ZnO (0.06 M), $\mathrm{{}}Z\mathrm{{nO}}$ (0.08 M), and $\mathrm{ZnO}\left(0.10\mathrm{M}\right)$ thin films are $1.42\mathrm{nm}$ , $0.58\mathrm{nm}$ , $0.55\mathrm{nm}$ , 0.65 nm, $2.41~\mathrm{nm}$ , and 4.63, respectively. The surface roughness of the $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ -treated $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ thin films is lower than that of the $\mathrm{{}}Z\mathrm{{nO}}$ thin films up to a concentration of $0.06{\bf M}$ and increases significantly at $0.08{\bf M}$ and $0.10\mathbf{M}$ . This phenomenon can be attributed to the different diffusion behaviors of the $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ solution at higher concentrations [46–49]. The scanning electron microscope images show that both the Ref. $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ and $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ $(0.06{\bf M})$ thin films have wrinkled network structures (Fig. S2). However, ZnO (0.06 M) thin films have a smoother surface than Ref. $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ due to the surface diffusion of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ molecules via $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ treatment, whereas Ref. $\mathrm{{}}Z\mathrm{{nO}}$ film consists of denser grains due to the agglomeration of $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ [47, 50, 51].
## 3.3  Influence of fluorine modification on chemical nature of the surface
XPS measurements were conducted to analyze the nature of chemical bonding and the chemical state of $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ thin films before and after $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ treatment. The survey spectrum (Fig. S1) of Ref. $Z\mathrm{nO}$ and $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ -treated $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ ( $\mathrm{\Delta}Z\mathrm{nO}$ (0.06 M)) thin films indicates that the $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ treatment leads to fluorine doping on the surface of the $Z\mathrm{nO}$ thin films. The XPS spectra of $Z\mathfrak{n}~2p$ for both $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ and $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ $(0.06{\bf M})$ (Fig. 4, left column) show two distinct peaks, which correspond to Zn $2p_{3/2}$ and $Z n2p_{1/2}$ , respectively. The energy difference between the peaks is ${\sim}23\ \mathrm{eV},$ characteristic of the divalent $(Z\mathfrak{n}^{2+})$ state of zinc in $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ [52]. The $Z\mathrm{n}~2p$ peaks of the ZnO ${\bf\Gamma}(0.06{\bf M})$ thin films are slightly shifted toward a higher binding energy $(0.6\ \mathrm{eV})$ compared with the values of the $Z\mathfrak{n}~2p$ peaks of Ref. $\mathrm{{}}Z\mathrm{{nO}}$ . This is due to the formation of Zn-OF and $Z\mathrm{nF}$ species on the $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ surface, which provides additional negative charges around the $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions [53, 54]. However, the energy difference between the peaks remains the same. This finding indicates that the $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ state is chemically stable and not affected by the $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ treatment [55]. The XPS spectra of $\mathrm{~O~}1s$ of the Ref. $Z\mathrm{nO}$ thin film (Fig. 4a, middle column) show three distinct peaks at $531.07\ \mathrm{eV}$ ${529.9\mathrm{eV}},\quad$ and ${529.3\mathrm{eV}}$ , corresponding to the chemisorbed oxygen species (such as hydroxyl $(\mathrm{-OH})$ group) on the $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ surface, oxygen vacancies, and $Z\mathrm{n-O}$ bond (i.e., $0^{2\cdot}$ ions of the wurtzite structure), respectively. A comparative analysis indicates that the oxygen vacancy formation is suppressed by the $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ treatment of the $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ thin films (Fig. 4b, middle column). This phenomenon can be attributed to the fluorine atoms occupying the oxygen vacancy site rather than the interstitial sites because of the lower formation energy of the former [56, 57]. The passivation of oxygen vacancy site by fluorine atoms eliminates the gap states, thereby lowering the leakage current in the devices [58]. Furthermore, the ratio of the chemisorbed oxygen peak to the Zn-O bond peak decreases after surface modification with $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ The reduction in the chemisorbed oxygen group is consistent with the FTIR spectroscopy results (Fig. 2a). The XPS spectra of F 1s for $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ $(0.06{\bf M})$ exhibit a fluorine signal (Fig. 4a, right column), whereas that of Ref. $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ do not exhibit a signal (Fig. 4b, right column). The XPS spectra of $\mathrm{~F~}1s$ for $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ and $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ $(0.06{\bf M})$ highlight that $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ treatment leads to the surface modification of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ through fluorination on the surface of $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ .
## 3.4  Influence of fluorine modification on surface properties
UPS measurements were obtained to estimate the work function shift after the $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ treatment of $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ . Figure 5a shows that the photoemission cutoff for $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ changes with the $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ concentration. The considerable change in the work function at a lower concentration $(0.02{\bf M})$ can likely be attributed to the interaction of fluorine with the oxygen vacancy and hydroxyl sites on the surface of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ [59]. The fluorine substitution at the oxygen vacancy and hydroxyl sites (as illustrated in the XPS results, Fig.  4) generates energy levels near the conduction band minimum of $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ [38], which reduces the work function of $\mathrm{{}}Z\mathrm{{nO}}$ . The electronegative fluorine atoms on the $\mathrm{{}}Z\mathrm{{nO}}$ surface tend to create a double layer with a negatively charged outer surface [60]. As the concentration increases, a dipole field that can oppose the electron transport from the bulk into vacuum is generated; thereby, the work function is increased [61]. Until the $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ concentration of $0.06{\mathrm{~M~}}$ , the work function of $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ shifts upward (Fig. 5b); however, beyond this concentration, the work function shifts downward with respect to that of the Ref. $\mathrm{{}}Z\mathrm{{nO}}$ thin film. These results indicate that the work function of the $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ thin films varies significantly with the $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ treatment, although the optical property is comparatively stable, as indicated by the transmittance spectra (Fig. 2b).
## 3.5  Influence of fluorine modification on device performance
To clarify the effect of $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ treatment on the resistance of the $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ , $I{-}V$ plots of $\mathrm{ITO/ZnO/}$ perovskite $\mathrm{(CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3})}/$ Au were evaluated, as shown in Fig. 6. The $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ treatment does not considerably influence the charge transport properties of $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ thin films. All the fabricated devices exhibit a linear behavior. The $I{-}V$ characteristics of the devices fabricated with $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ (0.04 M) and $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ (0.06 M) are similar to those of Ref. $Z\mathrm{nO}$ . This finding indicates that the treatment of $\mathrm{{}}Z\mathrm{{nO}}$ at an appropriate concentration does not significantly influence the charge transport properties of $\mathrm{{}}Z\mathrm{{nO}}$ . At concentrations lower $(0.02\mathbf{M})$ and higher ( $0.08{\bf M}$ and $0.10\mathbf{M},$ than the optimized concentration, the current decreases. The increase in the resistance to charge transport for $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ (0.02 M) can likely be attributed to the unfavorable energy level alignment between its fermi level $(3.7\mathrm{eV})$ and the conduction band of perovskite $(3.9\mathrm{eV})$ . The decrease in the charge transport at higher concentration may be due to the poor crystallization of the (0 0 2) plane, as indicated by the XRD results (Fig. 1). The resistivity of the $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ increases due to the deterioration of crystallization at a higher concentration of fluorine [62].
To clarify the effect of $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ -treated $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ on the photovoltaic performance, devices having a planar n-i-p configuration of $\mathrm{ITO/ZnO}$ ­ $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ -treated $\mathrm{ZnO)/CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}/}$ spiro-OMeTAD/Ag were fabricated and their photovoltaic performances were evaluated. The schematic of the fabricated perovskite solar cells (planar n-i-p configuration) is shown in Fig. 7a. The work function of $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ $(0.02{\bf M})$ is lower than that of the perovskite, whereas the work functions of $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ ${(0.04~\mathrm{M})}$ and $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ $(0.10\ \mathrm{M})$ are nearly the same as those of $\mathrm{ZnO}\left(0.06\mathrm{M}\right)$ and $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ (0.10 M), respectively. Considering the conclusions derived from UPS and $I{-}V$ measurements, ZnO $(0.06{\bf M})$ and $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ $\left(0.08{\bf M}\right)$ thin films were compared with $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ thin films (Fig. 7b). The valence band minimum of $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ was determined considering the UPS measurements. The energy levels of the perovskite, spiro-OMeTAD, and $\mathrm{Ag}$ were obtained from an existing report [63]. The photovoltaic performance of the fabricated devices is shown in Fig. 7c, and the corresponding photovoltaic parameters are listed in Table 1. The $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ treatment increases the PCE of $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ . The enhancement in the opencircuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ can be attributed to the passivation of surface defects upon surface modification [39]. The trend in the short-circuit current density $(J_{\mathrm{sc}})$ is a result of the energy level alignment and surface roughness of the $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ thin films. The downshift in the work function of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ $(0.06{\bf M})$ creates a driving force that favors the photoelectron transport from $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ to the $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ (0.06 M) ETL (Fig. 7b) [64].
Furthermore, the increased surface roughness obstructs the contact of the $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ $\left(0.08{\bf M}\right)$ thin film with $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ , which prevents the efficient transfer of the electrons at the $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ $(0.08\ \mathrm{M})/\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ interface [38, 65]. The photovoltaic performances of the fabricated perovskite solar cells are comparable to those of the reported perovskite solar cells with similar device architecture $\mathrm{(ZnO/CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}/}$ Spiro-meTAD/Ag), as shown in Table S1. Although the PCE obtained here is far behind the record performance, the present study introduces a cost-effective and facile strategy to develop $Z\mathrm{nO}.$ -based planar perovskite solar cells with improved stability. For reference, devices with higher performances can be achieved by the proper combination of the mesoscopic architecture having 3D perovskites with mixed cations and/or halides, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ or $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ ETL, and polymer additives [35, 66, 67]. Therefore, we believe that this paper contains very important information regarding the stability and degradation mechanism at the $\mathrm{ZnO/CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ interface, which could be a potential reference for thin $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ film–based planar optoelectronic devices.
The EIS and linear sweep voltammetry of the Ref. $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ and $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ $(0.06{\bf M})$ ETL-based perovskite solar cells were measured in dark conditions as they are effective methods to understand the charge carrier recombination and leakage currents in the device during the operation [68, 69]. Figure 8a shows the Nyquist plots of the device fabricated with Ref. $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ and $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ $(0.06{\bf M})$ . The inset shows the equivalent circuit used to analyze the Nyquist plots using EC lab software. The $R_{\mathrm{s}}$ represents the series resistance, $R_{\mathrm{{ct}}}$ and $C_{\mathrm{PE1}}$ represent the charge transfer resistance and its corresponding capacitance, and $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ and $C_{\mathrm{PE}2}$ correspond to the recombination resistance and its corresponding capacitance, respectively [11]. The smaller semicircle in the high-frequency region corresponds to the $R_{\mathrm{{ct}}}$ and $C_{\mathrm{PE1}}$ , and the large semicircle in the lower frequency region corresponds to the $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ and $C_{\mathrm{PE}2}$ . It can be clearly seen that the $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ of the ZnO (0.06 M) device $(2.52\times10^{5}\Omega)$ is larger than that of the Ref. ZnO–based device $(0.78\times10^{5}\Omega)$ . Furthermore, the recombination lifetime $(\tau_{\mathrm{rec}})$ can be derived from the $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ and $C_{\mathrm{PE}2}$ , using the formula of $\tau_{\mathrm{rec}}=R_{\mathrm{rec}}\:C_{\mathrm{PE}2}$ As the $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ of $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ (0.06 M) is greater than that of Ref. $\mathrm{znO}$ , the $\tau_{\mathrm{rec}}$ of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ (0.06 M) is greater than that of Ref. $Z\mathrm{nO}$ . This clearly indicates that $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ treatment effectively contributes to the reduction of the recombination losses, and thus improves the charge collection efficiency of the devices [58]. Figures 8b, c show the Nyquist plots of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ $(0.06{\bf M})$ and Ref. ZnO–based devices at different bias voltages of $0{-}0.8\mathrm{V}.$ The values of the $R_{\mathrm{{ct}}}$ and $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ decrease with an increase of the bias voltage (Fig. S3).
In addition, the magnitude of $R_{\mathrm{{ct}}}$ and $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ is reduced with increasing the bias voltage. These results indicate that the effect of detrimental back electrons at the $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}/$ perovskite interface is reduced via $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ treatment [14]. Figure  8d shows the curves of the perovskite solar cells based on the Ref. $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ and $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ (0.06 M). The current density of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ $(0.06{\bf M})$ –based devices $(1.126\upmu\mathrm{A}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ is nearly five times lower than that of Ref. ZnO–based devices $(4.915\upmu\mathrm{A}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ ). This indicates that $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ treatment effectively decreases the current leakage and reduces the charge recombination at the interfaces.
## 3.6  Influence of fluorine modification on chemical stability of perovskite
The enhancement in the chemical stability of the $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ upon $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ treatment at the $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}/\mathrm{ZnO}$ interface was verified using the XRD results obtained upon heating. As shown in Fig. 9, the $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ deposited on Ref. $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ thin films degrades upon heating within $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ , whereas the $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ deposited on the surface-modified $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ thin film (ZnO $\left(0.06{\bf M}\right)^{\cdot}$ ) is stable. The $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ deposited on the $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ is degraded owing to the generation of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ , as depicted by the XRD peak corresponding to $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ and change in the film color from brown to yellow. The appearance of a dominant $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ peak at $2\theta=12.67^{\circ}$ after heating shows that the $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ deposited on Ref. $\mathrm{{}}Z\mathrm{{nO}}$ degraded rapidly compared to that deposited on ZnO (0.06 M).
The improvement in the photovoltaic performance and perovskite stability is attributed to the fluorine atoms that can passivate the oxygen defects and replace the chemisorbed oxygen (-OH), as illustrated in Fig. 10. The $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ treatment of the $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ thin films suppresses the formation of oxygen vacancy due to its comparatively lower formation energy, which improves the open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ and reduces the leakage current. The increased stability of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ deposited on ZnO (0.06 M) thin films can be attributed to the reduced Lewis acidity of the $\mathrm{{}}Z\mathrm{{nO}}$ surface, resulting from the replacement of the chemisorbed oxygen (-OH) by fluorine atoms, thereby preventing the deprotonation of the $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ .
## 4  Conclusions
The surfaces of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ thin films were modified using $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ solution to alleviate the degradation of perovskite at $\mathrm{ZnO}/$ $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ , thereby enhancing the photovoltaic performance of perovskite solar cells. The influence of the $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ solution concentration on the physical and chemical properties of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ thin films was investigated. The surface fluorination tuned the work function of the $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ thin films without affecting the optical properties. The FTIR and XPS results confirmed that fluorine replaced the $-\mathrm{OH}$ terminal groups and passivated the oxygen defects on the surface of the $Z\mathrm{nO}$ thin films. This phenomenon prevented the $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ degradation and increased $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ . The increased surface roughness and unfavorable energy level alignment at a higher concentration resulted in a decrease in $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ . At the optimal $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ concentration (0.06 M), the PCE of the n-i-p planar perovskite-based solar cells fabricated using the surface-modified $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ thin films was three times higher than that of the cell fabricated with Ref. $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ as the ETL. These results highlighted that the surface modification of $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ thin films with $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ having an optimized concentration can enhance the photovoltaic performance and chemical stability. This study can provide reference for increasing the stability of perovskite through a simple surface modification of the charge transport layer, which is of significance for perovskite-based optoelectronic devices.
